<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agostino Depretis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agostino Depretis was an Italian statesman who served as prime minister several times between 1876 and 1887, leading the parliamentary Left after the fall of the Right in 1876. He became known for trasformismo, the practice of building majorities by absorbing deputies from the opposition. His governments widened the franchise in 1882, made primary education compulsory and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1882">Triple Alliance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agostino-depretis.jpg" alt="Agostino Depretis" width="250" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agostino_Depretis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Agostino Depretis was born on 31 January 1813 at Bressana Bottarone, near Stradella, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s French Empire. He graduated in law, became a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a> and took part in Mazzinian conspiracies, nearly being captured by the Austrians while smuggling arms into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Elected a deputy in 1848, he joined the Left and founded the newspaper Il Diritto. Although he opposed Camillo Benso di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>, Cavour made him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> in 1859.</p><p>In 1860 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to reconcile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>'s wish for immediate union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s plan to wait, and replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-crispi">Francesco Crispi</a> as pro-dictator, but he failed to persuade Garibaldi and resigned in September. He later served as minister of public works in 1862 and of the navy in 1866, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s defeat at Lissa made his tenure controversial. After the death of Urbano Rattazzi in 1873 he became leader of the Left.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The fall of Marco Minghetti's government in March 1876, called the parliamentary revolution, made Depretis prime minister on 25 March 1876 with the first cabinet composed only of the Left. In the November 1876 election the Left won 414 of 508 seats. The Coppino law of 15 July 1877 made primary education compulsory, secular and free for children aged six to nine. He resigned in December 1877 and again in March 1878, then served briefly in 1879, and returned as prime minister in 1881 after the French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>.</p><p>On 20 May 1882 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-alliance-1882">Triple Alliance</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. An electoral reform of 1882 lowered the voting age from 25 to 21 and raised the number of eligible voters from 621,896 to 2,017,829. From 1883 he moved to the right and brought in Minghetti's conservatives in the process known as Trasformismo, a flexible centrist coalition meant to isolate the extremes. His governments completed the railway system and began colonial policy with the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Despite gout, Depretis remained prime minister until his death at Stradella on 29 July 1887, aged 74. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-crispi">Francesco Crispi</a> replaced him. Critics held that Trasformismo sacrificed principle for short-term gain, bred corruption and widened the gap between parliamentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the real country, and that his rule raised indirect taxation and strained finances through extravagance in public works. His defenders point to the reforms of his governments, which widened suffrage and free education.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agostino Depretis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The liberal Right governed until 1876, after which the Left under <b>Agostino Depretis</b> practised trasformismo, absorbing opponents into shifting majorities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cava Manara, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Stradella, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.13,9.1&amp;year=1861" aria-label="Cava Manara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Agostino Depretis"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5547 6220h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5547 6220h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.13,9.1&amp;year=1861">What was here in 1861? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
